            "lens": "review-resilience",
            "findings": [],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all four changed paths in full. Worktree no longer reports 'taken' when git gave no answer. This covers a git that could not be started, was killed by a signal, or ran past its deadline, and it also covers a question that was never put to git. The change tightens failure handling and does not regress baseline behavior.",
              "answered() requires that the context is not expired, that the error is an ExitError, and that the exit code is non-negative. The check runs before cancel, via defer. A genuine git exit status such as 128 for 'not a repository' is still treated as an answer, so the path is reported as taken. That is the intended behavior.",
              "The default timeout of 5 minutes replaces the old unlimited behavior. For the local questions this adapter puts, it is a reasonable bound and removes a hang risk. The patch shows no evidence that any call legitimately exceeds 5 minutes, so a concrete failure mode cannot be established.",
              "The architecture_test.go change only reorders map entries and has no runtime effect. The new tests cover the timeout, not-found, signal, and late-exit-status cases, and they cover a real git given a nanosecond deadline."
            ]
          }
        }
